Title:
EDR/XDR Bypass and Detection Evasion Techniques: An Investigation of Advanced Evasion Strategies from a Red Team Perspective

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This article surveys modern Windows EDR/XDR detection surfaces and the evasion techniques attackers/red teams use to reduce telemetry and behavioral detections.  
- It explains how user-mode API hooking, ETW telemetry, AMSI/script logging, and kernel callbacks form a “closed-loop” detection pipeline that breaks many classic tradecraft (e.g., PowerShell cradles, Mimikatz, CreateRemoteThread injection).  
- Core evasion methods covered include API unhooking (restoring ntdll/k32 stubs), BOF/COFF in-memory execution to avoid process creation and disk artifacts, and direct/indirect syscalls to bypass user-mode hooks.  
- It also discusses ETW suppression/patching (e.g., EtwEventWrite tampering) and high-risk kernel callback evasion via kernel structure manipulation/driver abuse.  
- Beyond single techniques, it emphasizes layered combinations (e.g., indirect syscalls + ETW blinding) and “time-difference” (latency/race) attacks that exploit EDR analysis delays.  
- Useful for red teams and malware researchers designing post-exploitation tradecraft, and for blue teams to understand where to add integrity checks, kernel-level correlation, and detection for delayed execution primitives.

Technical Focus:
- User-mode API hooking and API unhooking (ntdll/kernel32 inline/IAT hooks)
- BOF/COFF in-memory execution and custom loaders (fileless tradecraft)
- Direct vs indirect syscalls and call-stack/sequence evasion
- ETW provider telemetry, EtwEventWrite patching, and logging suppression
- Kernel callbacks (PsSetCreate*NotifyRoutine, image/thread/process notify) and evasion risks
- Temporal/latency (“time-difference”) evasion using timers/suspended threads/APCs

Use Cases:
- Build or evaluate post-exploitation loaders that minimize process creation and disk I/O
- Test EDR resilience against unhooking + syscall-based injection primitives
- Develop detections for ETW tampering, suspicious memory permission transitions, and syscall anomalies
- Red-team validation of EDR blind spots using controlled multi-technique chains
- Threat hunting playbooks focused on delayed execution primitives (timers/APCs/threadpool)

Keywords:
EDR, XDR, Windows internals, API hooking, inline hooking, IAT hooking, API unhooking, ntdll.dll, kernel32.dll, ETW, EtwEventWrite, AMSI, Script Block Logging, BOF, COFF, Cobalt Strike, PostExpKit, in-memory execution, direct syscalls, indirect syscalls, kernel callbacks, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, process injection, VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory, CreateRemoteThread, stack walking, sleep obfuscation, time-difference attack, SetTimer, NtResumeThread, HVCI, Credential Guard